course: dictionary
lesson: 5160
title: Words that mean no going back
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you that a choice can never be undone. Irrevocable is one of them. The ir- at the start means not, as it does in irregular, so the word points to something that cannot be taken back.
explain: Read the clues around the word: «Take the Adventure, heed the call, now ere the irrevocable moment passes!» Ere means before, so the moment is about to go, and once it has gone it cannot be taken back. Try your meaning in the sentence: "the moment that can never be taken back" still makes sense.
explain: Irrevocable is a formal word. You will meet it in serious writing, in law, and in stories when a character takes a big step. An irrevocable promise tells the reader there is no going back. Some words said in anger can feel irrevocable, so think before you speak.
fact: Irrevocable means not able to be changed, cancelled or taken back once it is done.
fact: An irrevocable decision is one you cannot undo.
fact: The ir- at the start means not, as in irregular.
fact: «Ere» means before, so «ere the irrevocable moment passes» means before the moment is gone for good.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: What does irrevocable mean? | Very small and light | *Not able to be changed, cancelled or taken back | Able to be changed or cancelled later | Brightly coloured
exercise: An irrevocable step suggests what? | A step that can be fixed later | A step on a staircase | A quick and easy step | *There is no going back
exercise: In «the irrevocable moment passes», which word means before? | Adventure | heed | call | *ere
test: Which of these is most likely an irrevocable decision? | *Signing a contract that cannot be cancelled | Picking a book for tonight | Changing your mind about a game | Choosing a snack
test: Why might a child think carefully before saying hurtful words? | *Because some words, once said, can feel irrevocable | Because such words are always easy to take back | Because hurtful words are too quiet to hear | Because irrevocable means funny
exam: Jayden says irrevocable is just a fancy word for sad. Is he right? | No, it means shiny and bright | Yes, it means quiet and gloomy | Yes, it means very unhappy | *No, it means not able to be changed, cancelled or taken back
